RED TEAM TEST 1a — clean-signal gap (PASS as finding)
DATE: 2026-08-15
INPUT: 1120-byte file, 3 IAST chars, pure English/OCR noise + page headers
EXPECTED: clean=False (garbage should be rejected)
ACTUAL:   clean=True, reason="looks like clean Sanskrit"
ROOT CAUSE: source_ready._clean_signal — the size-1000-2000 band with low sanskrit
  density (<50 chars) is NOT caught. The two checks are:
    - sanskrit_chars < 50 AND size > 2000  → not clean
    - size < 1000                          → not clean
  GAP: 1000 <= size <= 2000 AND sanskrit_chars < 50 → falls through to clean=True
SEVERITY: HIGH (garbage OCR routed to CLEAN_ETEXT → TRANSLATE)
FIX: add a density ratio check: sanskrit_chars/size < threshold → not clean,
  regardless of absolute size.

RED TEAM TEST 1b — format-detection false-positive (PASS as finding)
INPUT: the same garbage (3 IAST chars in 1120 bytes of English)
ACTUAL:   format=RAW_SANSKRIT, why="raw Sanskrit (iast)"
ROOT CAUSE: assess._scheme returns "iast" if ANY diacritic char present (no density
  threshold). 3 chars in 1120 → still called IAST Sanskrit.
SEVERITY: MEDIUM-HIGH (garbage mislabeled as RAW_SANSKRIT → compounds finding 1a)
FIX: scheme detection needs a minimum IAST/Devanagari density before declaring RAW_SANSKRIT.
